<section class="almanac-section mental-model-section">
  <div class="shell mental-model-grid">
    <div class="mental-model-copy">
      <p class="almanac-kicker">The 90-second model</p>
      <h2>Six facts explain the architecture.</h2>
      <p>Dekopon’s maturity is in its seams. Keep these distinctions straight and the codebase becomes much easier to reason about.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="mental-model-list">
      <li><span>01</span><div><strong>The model gets one tool, not one tool per capability.</strong><p>Its script composes only session-granted operations through a pure Rust interpreter.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>02</span><div><strong>Direct mode and broker mode are intentionally different.</strong><p>The direct linker is empty and read-only; the broker linker exposes one project-owned HTTP contract after authorization.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>03</span><div><strong>A component import is a requirement, not a permission.</strong><p>The broker still binds exact destination, method, calls, bytes, and deadline to one invocation.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>04</span><div><strong>A proposal is not authority.</strong><p>Identity and policy never arrive as client fields. The broker derives context from Unix peer credentials and trusted configuration.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Every operation starts fresh.</strong><p>Compiled components are reused, but each describe or invoke receives a new bounded Store and instance.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>06</span><div><strong>Audit, traces, and logs answer different questions.</strong><p>Authorization is hash-linked; timing is traced; accounting and exceptional facts are logged.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

